# Brief: critical temperature of the 2D Ising model from a three-stage simulation study

Kept by `experiment-repo` (context, plan, analysis). Source: the `Research objective` issue
(`experiment-repo`#1) and its frozen material `/initial-state/inputs/0/ising.py`
(sha256 `88bd6931eb74df0f85ed8cd23138e9c058ebeea3b9e6c28f10ec0628a42eb4c0`).

## The task (quoted)

> Locate the critical temperature T_c of the two-dimensional Ising model (square lattice, periodic
> boundaries, J = 1, no field) from simulations made with the given program
> `/initial-state/inputs/0/ising.py` (numpy only; `python3 ising.py --help`), and compare it with the
> exact value 2/ln(1+sqrt(2)) = 2.2692.

> The study has three stages; each is designed from the result of the one before:
>
> 1. Pilot: L = 64, T from 1.5 to 3.5 in steps of 0.1, seeds 1 and 2, 20,000 measured sweeps after
>    2,000 burn-in. Locate the peak of the susceptibility.
> 2. Crossing: L = 32, 64 and 128 at 9 equally spaced temperatures from 0.12 below to 0.12 above the
>    pilot's peak, seeds 1 to 3, 20,000 sweeps, keeping the time series (`--series`). Estimate T_c
>    where the Binder cumulants of the three sizes cross, with a bootstrap 95% interval over seeds,
>    and the integrated autocorrelation time of |m| at each point, to say whether 20,000 sweeps are
>    enough.
> 3. Check: L = 256 at 5 equally spaced temperatures from 0.03 below to 0.03 above the stage-2
>    estimate, seeds 1 and 2, 10,000 sweeps. Say whether the L = 256 Binder values agree with the
>    stage-2 crossing.

> Deliver the data and analysis code of every stage, two figures (the pilot's susceptibility and
> heat capacity against T; the Binder cumulants of all sizes near the crossing), and a one-page
> report `report/report.md` (Markdown is enough) with T_c, its interval, the comparison with the
> exact value and what stage 3 showed. On this machine a single stage takes several minutes or more.

> How the report is written is part of this task: the report states each stage's result as soon as
> it is known. paper-repo opens one request in experiment-repo per stage (stage 1, then stage 2, then
> stage 3), each asking for that stage's numbers, and opens the next one as soon as the previous one
> has been answered, not before.

